    "short_description": "User Datagram Protocol is a communication protocol that transmits data in the form of individual datagrams or packets over a network.",
    "full_description": "UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is a connectionless, best-effort delivery protocol. It provides simple and lightweight communication between applications by sending and receiving data in small units called datagrams. UDP does not guarantee reliability; it simply forwards the packet with no error checking or congestion control.

UDP operates at the transport layer of the OSI model, specifically in end-to-end mode where packets are sent from a sender to a receiver through an intermediary computer (a router). The data is divided into segments and encapsulated within datagrams that are transmitted over IP networks. Each segment contains the source address and destination address for routing purposes.

UDP is simple in design; it lacks features such as error checking, flow control, and connection establishment/disconnection. This makes it suitable for low-bandwidth environments or applications where reliability isn't critical. Examples of UDP-based protocols include DNS (Domain Name System), DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol), and the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).",

